WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.600
Hi everyone.

2
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:05.720
I'm very excited to be the one to start
this panel of my presentation

3
00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:10.440
is titled Poetry of Movement, Affect and Virality in Warsan Shire’s Home.

4
00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:15.640
I'm going to
look at the process of virality

5
00:00:15.640 --> 00:00:20.880
when something and more specifically
a poem, goes viral in digital spaces.

6
00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:24.920
How does that impact its reception,
the way that people react to it?

7
00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:28.640
So the ethos of movement that lie
at the heart of internet virality,

8
00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:32.280
interacting complicated ways
with the spatial and temporal esthetics

9
00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:37.080
of digital platforms, producing new
literary, affective and political regimes.

10
00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:41.520
When a text goes viral on the internet,
the questions that I'm asking is

11
00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:46.120
what happens to the impact generated by it
as it is repeatedly encountered?

12
00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:50.040
Does it lose its sense of uniqueness
and creativity upon subsequent readings?

13
00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:53.880
Or alternatively, does it acquire
new layers of meaning in the process?

14
00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:57.400
Given the dizzying pace
of viral phenomena, it is quite difficult

15
00:00:57.400 --> 00:01:00.560
to situate them
within traditional critical frameworks.

16
00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:04.800
For the purpose of this presentation,
I will use movement as an entry point

17
00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:06.480
into studying viral poems

18
00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:09.840
and how they produce meaning and affect
within digital spaces.

19
00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:12.840
In the case of internet poetry.

20
00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:15.120
The sense of spatiality
is significantly different

21
00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:16.080
as the fictional

22
00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:20.560
and materiality of the page and the book
is replaced by the fluidity of the screen.

23
00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:23.960
While there have been multiple critics
written about this,

24
00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:27.280
for the purpose of this presentation,
I'm looking at Anna Munster,

25
00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:30.840
who studies the figure of the clone
as being emblematic of contemporary

26
00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:35.360
digital culture, drawing attention away
from the sameness of clones.

27
00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:40.800
Munster focuses instead on the genealogy
of mutations, fluctuations and failures

28
00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:44.320
that constituted serial history, thereby

29
00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:49.080
drawing attention to the unacknowledged
role of differential relations

30
00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:53.720
in arranging, sequencing, and replicating
the codes of digital culture.

31
00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:56.840
Digital space,
then, is characterized by this continuous

32
00:01:56.840 --> 00:02:01.000
serial repetition, albeit
with variable differentials.

33
00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:04.560
It's composed of an ensemble of different
elements, and hence the manner

34
00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:08.440
in which these combinations form,
interact, and impact upon each other.

35
00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:12.000
That data means the experience
of inhabiting digital spaces.

36
00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:15.960
Poetry is also one of the elements
that enters into a combination

37
00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:19.240
with the others, borrowing
from the model of the clone

38
00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:21.720
and the series of repetitions
engendered by it.

39
00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:25.160
One can argue that the experience
of reading a poem in digital space

40
00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:28.880
is characterized by repetition,
but each encounter is slightly different

41
00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:32.720
owing to the constantly
changing nature of constituent elements.

42
00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:36.120
This lack of fixing
this means that such poetry remains

43
00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:39.240
lodged in the space between the familiar
and the unfamiliar,

44
00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:43.040
potentially producing
a sense of disoriented or uncanny ness.

45
00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:46.720
While the act of rereading
can also be done with print based poetry,

46
00:02:46.760 --> 00:02:49.480
it requires conscious volition
on the part of the reader.

47
00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:51.160
In the case of digital ecology,

48
00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:54.600
it is built into the very network
architecture of the space itself.

49
00:02:54.760 --> 00:02:58.680
The algorithm that defines these spaces
facilitate and rewards

50
00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:02.480
repetitive encounters with texts
and narratives, unintentionally,

51
00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:05.480
perhaps producing an uncanny reading
atmosphere.

52
00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:10.760
This slightly unsettling digital space
also translates to the poetic subject

53
00:03:10.760 --> 00:03:15.560
being rendered uncanny, as a poem travels
to the labor and time pathways

54
00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:17.520
generated by digital algorithms,

55
00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:21.840
the poetic subject is repeatedly
reinterpreted and remediated.

56
00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:24.840
In some ways, digital space accentuates

57
00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:29.120
the traditional role of deformities
often ascribed to poetry itself.

58
00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:33.600
It stimulates new ways of thinking
and feeling about the subject.

59
00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:38.760
As readers encounter the poem through
junctions, junctures, flows, and networks.

60
